{"qid":"Q10957559","names":["Principality of Moldavia"],"title":"Principality of Moldavia","endonym":{"native":"Țara Moldovei","lang":"ro"},"capsule":"Moldavia was a medieval principality between the Eastern Carpathians and the Dniester, founded circa 1359 by Bogdan I, who broke away from Hungarian suzerainty to establish an independent Orthodox Christian state. Under Stephen the Great (r. 1457–1504) it reached its apex, repelling Ottoman, Polish, and Hungarian invasions and building a network of monastery-fortresses that survive today; after Stephen's death the principality gradually accepted Ottoman suzerainty, paying tribute while retaining an elected ruling class. From the 17th century, Greek Phanariot princes appointed by Istanbul governed the principality, deepening its dependence; Moldavia finally emerged from Ottoman and Russian competition when it united with Wallachia in 1859 to form the nucleus of modern Romania.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core border zones are reasonably attested through Ottoman census registers, Polish diplomatic correspondence, and Moldavian chronicles; eastern boundaries along the Dniester shifted frequently and are subject to interpretive variation across modern historical atlases.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Moldavia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Moldavia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Moldavia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moldavia"}],"britannica":"place/Moldavia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Moldavia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q946073","name":"Bogdan I of Moldavia","start":1363,"end":1367,"image":"Bogdan.I.pictura.jpg","imageUrl":"https://c